From c9de43286d76f538c26b3de9e706cc97239263af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaden <kaden@localhost>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 18:10:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] cuda: give each warp its own rows on the multi-column
 mmvq path

Speculative decoding verifies several draft tokens in one batched
forward, which lands on mul_mat_vec_q with ncols_dst > 1 -- 37% of GPU
time in an MTP run, at 196us a call against 99us for the one-column
kernel that streams the same weights. Everything done for decode so far
was gated to ncols_dst == 1, so this path was untouched.

The activation is re-read by every block of the grid, and past one column
its traffic dominates: ~102 MB per matmul against 21 MB of weights. That
is volume, not access pattern -- staging it in shared memory the way the
one-column path does gains ~1% and costs the shared memory the real fix
needs. The fix is reuse: more output rows per block.

Registers rather than shared memory limit how many rows fit (REG:200,
28 inlined dot products live at once), so give each warp its own rows and
have every warp walk the whole of K instead of the warps splitting K and
sharing every row. Per-thread accumulators are unchanged, a block covers
four times the rows, and the cross-warp reduction disappears.

Also clamps the row used for addressing: a block covers its full row
count whether the tensor has that many left or not, and the staging was
reading off the end of the weights. Two rows got away with it, eight
would not.

pp512 (b=7 ub=7) 61.08 -> 82.12 t/s; kernel 188 -> 129 us.
MTP decode 32.94 -> 37.9 t/s, a 1.27x speculative speedup against 1.11x.
Single-token decode is untouched at ~29.9.

test-backend-ops MUL_MAT 1193/1193. Perplexity through this path (-b 7
-ub 7): 3.6199 +/- 0.084 against 3.6237 +/- 0.084 for the stock geometry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AaVsfEiPALnEHpNH9UZbjk
